  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Tuesday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: \n\nLarge upper level closed low pressure system is continuing to \nmove along the California coast today, before making is turn \ninland tonight. This low is expected to weaken as it begins to \nlift into Nevada Wednesday and Thursday with the low opening up \ninto a broad trough. By Friday morning most of the main energy \nwill shift east into Utah as the trough splits into two pieces, \nwith the northern half lifting into Montana and Alberta and a \nsouthern piece re-organizing into another closed low that will \ndrift from Arizona into the four corners region Saturday. This low\nto trough passage will bring several periods of isolated to \nscattered showers and thunderstorms beginning tonight and lasting \nthrough Friday morning. Light showers will develop this evening \nover southern Nevada, expanding in coverage as it moves north. \nWednesday and Thursday will see the best coverage for showers and \nstorms as the main energy of the system passes overhead. \nThunderstorms will be possible for eastern Nevada, with a few \nsevere storms possible for eastern White Pine, and Elko counties \nas the best CAPE and mid level lapse rates will be co-located with\nthe upper level lift. The SPC has this region high lighted for a \nmarginal risk of severe thunderstorms with gusty outflow winds as \nthe primary hazard. By Friday morning cooler air aloft will try \nand filter in, and snow levels which have been near 9000 feet for \nmajority of time will drop to about 6000 feet just as the \nprecipitation will be ending across northern Nevada. Timing may \nallow for snowfall which had been restricted to the mountain \nsummits to reach some of the higher elevation passes on NV225 \nFriday morning, as total look to range from a trace to 0.2\u201d. \nHowever the range peaks look to see the best snow chances with up \nto a 18\u201d possible above 9000 feet across central and northern NV. \nRainfall amounts look to be good with this system as most location\nlook to pick up from 0.25\u201d to 0.75\u201d in the valleys with up to \n2.00\u201d of liquid for the mountains through Friday afternoon. After \nFriday high pressure looks to quickly build in on the heels of the\ntrough passage, with the ridge firmly in control by Sunday, with \ncontinued ridging lasting through Wednesday of next week. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: \n \nThere is high confidence for the potential for isolated to \nscattered showers and thunderstorms starting this afternoon, and \npersisting on and off through Friday morning.\n\nThere is high confidence in the return of upper ridging Saturday \nthrough Wednesday of next week.",
